<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Westminster-Ardtornish</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Westminster-Ardtornish was a secret agreement made in 1462 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iv-of-england">Edward IV of England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-islay-earl-of-ross">John MacDonald II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-the-isles">Lord of the Isles</a>, and the exiled James Douglas, 9th Earl of Douglas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was to be conquered and divided, the Lord of the Isles and his kinsmen taking the north and Douglas the south, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its discovery led to John's loss of the earldom of Ross in 1475.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-westminster-ardtornish.jpg" alt="Treaty of Westminster-Ardtornish" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Mephiston999 at English Wikipedia. · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ardtornish_Castle_ruins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the minority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-scotland">James III of Scotland</a> the Scottish crown took the Lancastrian side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> by welcoming the fugitive Henry VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, who was then in the keeping of James's mother, Mary of Guelders. Edward IV of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> sought to reduce the threat of the exiled king by forming alliances with disaffected English and Scottish nobles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Earl of Douglas and his brother John Douglas of Balvenie carried Edward's proposals to the west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The highland lords gave their assent at Ardtornish Castle on 19 October 1461 and sent Ranald of the Isles and Duncan, Archdeacon of the Isles, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> as their envoys. The articles were finalised and sealed at Westminster Palace on 13 February 1462, and Edward IV signed them on 17 March. John of Islay, Earl of Ross, Donald Balagh and his son and heir John, with all the people of Ross and the Isles, were to become Edward's subjects at Whitsunday.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The agreement was followed by an attack by the Earl of Ross on crown lands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a> in 1462 and 1463. The Scottish crown made its own alliance with Edward IV by the treaty of York in 1464. When the English court revealed the agreement in 1475, John was summoned for treason, the charges including leagues and bands with Edward IV and the banished Earl of Douglas, and he calmed matters only by giving up Ross, which then included Skye.</p><p>In 1491 his half-nephew tried to recover Ross in the Raid on Ross, which the Scottish king used as grounds for abolishing the Lordship of the Isles. Historians have judged the treaty differently. Andrew Lang described it as an attempt to stab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in the back with a Celtic dirk, while Norman Macdougall thought its significance had been overplayed by such earlier writers. The Douglases were at that time generally the leaders of the pro-English party in Scotland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Westminster (1462)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John MacDonald II's secret <b>Treaty of Westminster-Ardtornish</b> with Edward IV in 1462 planned to partition Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-the-isles">Lordship of the Isles</a></li></ul></section>
